  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough next Tuesday...\n\nAbove average temperatures and dry conditions will prevail through\nFriday with zonal flow being dominant. Elevated fire weather\nconditions are expected Friday with critical fire weather\nconditions being possible. 500 mb troughing is expected to drop\ninto southern British Columbia Friday setting up a surface low\npressure system over southern Alberta/Saskatchewan. This setup is\nexpected to put much of Montana into the dry slot with downsloping\n700 mb winds. High temperatures in the 80s F with minimum relative\nhumidity values in the teens are likely. Winds gusting into 20s \nmph across much of the region with 30s and 40s mph for the western\nfoothills are expected. \n\nA pattern change over the weekend will bring cooler and wetter\nconditions through early next week. Ensemble systems are in good\nagreement that upper level troughing will dive south along the\nwest coast. Deterministic models are showing a low pressure \nsystem setting up over Montana and the southern Canadian \nprairies. As the low pressure system moves off to the east, it \nwill bring a cold front across Montana Saturday. This cold front \nwill advect in Pacific moisture and cooler temperatures.\nTemperatures Saturday are highly uncertain given the unknown\nfrontal timing. Locations near Judith gap could see high\ntemperatures in the 50s F on the low end while locations in far \nSoutheastern Montana and North Central Wyoming may still \nexperience much above average temperatures in the 80s F on the \nhigh end. There is much higher confidence in widespread below \naverage temperatures Sunday with most locations seeing highs below\n50 F. \n\nThere is even more uncertainty regarding precipitation and \nprecipitation type, though there are a few general statements \nthat can be made. Locations near the mountains and foothills will \nreceive more precipitation and have a better chance at seeing \nsnowfall and higher snowfall totals. Locations west of Rosebud\nCounty have a 50-80% chance of getting greater than 0.5 inches of\nprecipitation with locations to the east having a 20-50% chance.\nThe chances for greater than 1 inch of snow over the lower\nelevations from Yellowstone County and west are in the 40-70%\nrange. Precipitation chances increase Saturday behind the cold\nfront and begin to decrease throughout the day Monday. Stay tuned\nto the forecast over the coming days as confidence increases and \nthe forecast becomes more clear. Torgerson",
